// Cycle of Life: Realistic Fauna — Hieronymus Reptiles Collection.
//
// The biggest pack we tune: ~164 real species of monitor lizard, tortoise, gecko, bearded dragon,
// salamander, newt and frog, spread over 13 mod domains. Three things shape this file.
//
// 1. GROUP BASELINES, NOT 164 OPINIONS. The simulation cares that a Komodo dragon is not a Timor
//    monitor and that a Galapagos tortoise is not a Chaco tortoise; it does not care that two
//    near-identical brown frogs differ by a day. So each genus gets one baseline lifecycle, and
//    only bodyWeight (and lifespan, for the tortoises) is set per species — which is the value
//    that actually matters, since weight drives forage demand, grazing range, territory radius,
//    roaming speed and the satiation reserve pool. Same approach as fotsa-canids.json.
//
// 2. LIFE STAGES ARE NOT SEPARATE POPULATIONS. This mod gives eggs, juveniles and babies their own
//    spawn conditions, so each would otherwise be discovered as an independent breeding species —
//    a monitor would be modelled three times over (egg + juvenile + adult) and a tortoise five
//    times, inflating every population several-fold and, absurdly, having EGGS breed. Every
//    non-adult stage is therefore marked "chainSeed": true, which means "observe only": we do not
//    veto its spawns, do not simulate it as a population, and do not cull it. The mod keeps
//    producing hatchlings on its own terms and the ADULT is the population we actually manage.
//
// 3. PREDATOR STATUS IS LEFT TO AUTO-DISCOVERY. The mod authors did this properly: big monitors
//    hunt chicken/hare/frogs/raccoons, mid ones hunt chicken/hare/frogs, small ones hunt only
//    babies and frogs, and the real-world frugivorous Philippine monitors (olivaceus, mabitang,
//    bitatawa) hunt nothing at all. Discovery reads those lists correctly, so this file does not
//    override isPredator anywhere. Their hunting is already gated on the mod's own "saturated"
//    emotion state, which is the same idea as our satiation model, so no huntWhenFull floor
//    either: a full monitor stops hunting, which is exactly right for a gorge-and-fast reptile.
//
// 4. TORTOISES ARE SPLIT BY SEX. chelonoidis-male and chelonoidis-female both carry spawn
//    conditions, and the sex is in the entity code rather than on a "gender" axis, so each
//    tortoise becomes two species. Both halves get half the intended carryingCapacity so the
//    pair adds back up to one normal population (same fix as cats.json).
//
// Reptile and amphibian lifecycles in general: long lives, seasonal clutches, and juvenile
// mortality so high that a huge clutch still only replaces the parents. Litter sizes below are
// SURVIVING hatchlings, not egg counts — a frog laying 2000 eggs is modelled as a dozen young
// with 90% juvenile mortality, which is what the population actually experiences.
//
// VERIFICATION STATUS: the species keys in this file were derived from the mod's own entity
// JSON (its spawn conditions and variant axes), not read off a live server roster — this mod
// is not installed on the pack author's server. A key that turned out wrong does not break
// anything: an overlay entry that matches no species is simply ignored, and the animal keeps
// its auto-discovered values. If you run this mod, `/col species` (or the roster dumped to the
// server log at startup) lists the authoritative keys, and anything mistyped here will be
// absent from it. Corrections welcome.
{
